#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:6
msgid "Pattern Matching"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:8
msgid ""
"You can match on exact, literal tokens inside macro patterns. This is "
"extremely useful for building custom DSLs or structured syntax matching:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:12
msgid "// Copyright 2026 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:13
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:16
msgid "\"Starting...\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:17
msgid "\"Stopping...\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:29
msgid ""
"In this example, the tokens `start`, `stop`, and `restart` are matched "
"exactly as literal identifiers. Literal identifiers and custom symbols (such "
"as `=>`, `,`, `:`, etc.) can be sequenced to design readable and mnemonic "
"input syntax for your macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:36
msgid ""
"Note that the macro in the example invokes itself recursively in its "
"transcriber body."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:38
msgid ""
"Point out that because macros parse token streams, standard Rust rules (like "
"variables needing to be declared or modules needing to exist) do not apply "
"to the literal tokens being matched. They are treated purely as structural "
"matches."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/pattern-matching.md:42
msgid ""
"Macros can invoke themselves and other macros, which can be useful for some "
"more advanced macro design patterns which will be discussed later."
msgstr ""
